---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';
import { course } from '../../data/itc120.js';
import { hi, hiN } from '../../lib/code.js';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const vars = String.raw`String name = "Ana";
int score = 92;
double average = 85.0;
boolean passed = true;`;
const assign = String.raw`int x = 5;          // x starts at 5
x = x + 2;          // x becomes 7
int total = x * 3;  // total becomes 21`;
const cond = String.raw`if (score >= 75) {
  passed = true;
} else {
  passed = false;
}`;
const loop = String.raw`for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
  System.out.println(i);   // prints 0, 1, 2, 3
}`;
const method = String.raw`static int larger(int a, int b) {
  if (a > b) return a;
  return b;
}`;
const trace = String.raw`int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
  total = total + i;
}
System.out.println(total);`;

const quiz = [
  { q: 'After <code>int x = 5;</code> and then <code>x = x + 2;</code>, what is in <code>x</code>?', opts: ['5', '2', '7', 'An error: x cannot equal x + 2'], a: 2, why: 'The <code>=</code> sign stores, it does not compare. Java first works out the right side (5 + 2 = 7), then stores 7 in <code>x</code>.' },
  { q: 'What does <code>17 % 5</code> give in Java?', opts: ['3', '2', '3.4', '85'], a: 1, why: '<code>%</code> gives the <b>remainder</b>: 17 ÷ 5 = 3 remainder <b>2</b>.' },
  { q: 'What does this print? <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) System.out.println(i);</code>', opts: ['0, 1, 2', '1, 2, 3', '0, 1, 2, 3', '3'], a: 0, why: 'Start at 0; keep going while <code>i &lt; 3</code>. So <code>i</code> is 0, 1, 2. At 3 the condition is false and the loop stops.' },
  { q: 'What does <code>larger(4, 9)</code> return?', opts: ['4', '9', '13', 'true'], a: 1, why: '4 goes into <code>a</code> and 9 into <code>b</code>. <code>a &gt; b</code> is false, so the method returns <code>b</code>: <b>9</b>.' },
  { q: 'Which array index holds the <b>third</b> value?', opts: ['3', '2', '1', '0'], a: 1, why: 'Counting starts at 0: index 0 is the first value, index 1 the second, index 2 the third.' },
  { q: 'The program runs, but the answer is wrong. What kind of error is this?', opts: ['Syntax error', 'Runtime error', 'Logic error', 'No error'], a: 2, why: 'A <b>logic error</b>: the code follows Java\'s rules and does not crash, but the steps are wrong (for example, a wrong condition).' },
  { q: 'You forgot a <code>;</code> and the code will not compile. What kind of error is this?', opts: ['Syntax error', 'Runtime error', 'Logic error'], a: 0, why: 'A <b>syntax error</b> breaks Java\'s grammar, so the program never starts.' },
];
---
<Base title="Programming Refresher · ITC 120" description="A quick Java review before data structures and algorithms: variables, expressions, conditions, loops, methods, arrays, trace tables, and the three kinds of errors." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Prelim › Programming refresher</p>
    <p class="kicker">Introduction · Lesson 1 of 2</p>
    <h1>Programming Refresher</h1>
    <p class="lede">A quick Java review before data structures. Skip any part you already know.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>read a line like <code>int score = 92;</code> aloud, and explain what <code>=</code> does;</li>
        <li>predict what short code with conditions, loops, and methods prints;</li>
        <li>find a value in an array by its index (counting from 0);</li>
        <li>trace a loop with a trace table;</li>
        <li>tell syntax, runtime, and logic errors apart.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> your Programming 1 and 2 notes (if you have them), and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="blocks">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The building blocks</p>
      <h2>Rebuild the mental model first</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Before data structures, let's remember how programs store values, make decisions, repeat work, and reuse code. Every data structure in this course is built and used with these same pieces, so here are the seven things you need.</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>1. Variables are named places for values</h3>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Variable</p><p>A named place in memory that holds one value. Its <b>type</b> says what kind of value it can hold.</p></div>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(vars)} /></pre>
        <p>Read each line as <b>type name = value</b>. The type says what kind of value fits: <code>int</code> for whole numbers, <code>double</code> for decimals, <code>boolean</code> for true or false, <code>String</code> for text. A clear name (<code>score</code>, not <code>s</code>) tells the reader what the value means.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>2. Assignment stores; expressions calculate</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(assign)} /></pre>
        <p>The <code>=</code> sign is <b>assignment</b>: "work out the right side, then store the result in the variable on the left." It does not mean "is equal to." That is why <code>x = x + 2</code> makes sense: take the old <code>x</code> (5), add 2, and store 7 back in <code>x</code>.</p>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Expression</p><p>Anything Java can work out to one value, such as <code>x + 2</code> or <code>x * 3</code>.</p></div>
        <p>These operators build expressions:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code> <code>*</code></td><td>add, subtract, multiply</td><td><code>7 * 3</code> gives 21</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>divide (two <code>int</code>s drop the decimal part)</td><td><code>7 / 2</code> gives 3, but <code>7.0 / 2</code> gives 3.5</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>remainder after dividing</td><td><code>7 % 2</code> gives 1; <code>17 % 5</code> gives 2</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>3. Conditions let a program choose a path</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(cond)} /></pre>
        <p>If the score is 75 or higher, one path runs; otherwise, the other. Comparisons use <code>==</code> (equal), <code>!=</code> (not equal), <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>. Each comparison gives <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</p>
        <p class="small muted">Good to know: to compare two <code>String</code>s, use <code>name.equals("Ana")</code>, not <code>==</code>. With text, <code>==</code> checks whether both are the very same object in memory, so it can say <code>false</code> even when the letters match.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>4. Loops repeat a block of work</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(loop)} /></pre>
        <p>A <b>loop</b> runs the same lines again and again, with a new value of <code>i</code> each round. A <code>for</code> loop has three controls: where to <b>start</b> (<code>i = 0</code>), when to <b>keep going</b> (<code>i &lt; 4</code>), and how to <b>update</b> after each round (<code>i++</code>).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>5. A method performs one reusable task</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(method)} /></pre>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Method</p><p>A named block of code that does one task. Its <b>parameters</b> are the inputs it receives; its <b>return value</b> is the output it gives back.</p></div>
        <p>Think of a method as a small machine: <code>larger(3, 8)</code> puts 3 into <code>a</code> and 8 into <code>b</code>, and the machine gives back 8. Methods matter in this course because every operation on a data structure is a method, such as <code>push()</code>, <code>pop()</code>, and <code>insert()</code>, and we can test each one on its own.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>6. An array is a row of numbered slots</h3>
        <div class="mini-arr" aria-label="An array with values 19, 3, 42, 7, 56 at indexes 0 to 4">
          {[19, 3, 42, 7, 56].map((v, i) => <span class={i === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}><b>{v}</b><i>{i}</i></span>)}
        </div>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Array and index</p><p>An <b>array</b> is a fixed number of slots, side by side, that all hold the same type of value. Each slot's numbered position is its <b>index</b>.</p></div>
        <p>Counting starts at <b>0</b>, so <code>arr[2]</code> is the <b>third</b> slot: 42. The array is the first data structure of this course: you will see it again in <a href={base + '/itc120/intro/'}>Introduction to DSA</a>, and it gets a whole lesson in <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>Arrays</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>7. Trace code one line at a time</h3>
        <div class="two-col">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(trace)} /></pre>
          <table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Round</th><th>i</th><th>total</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>A <b>trace table</b> shows how each variable changes, step by step. Output: <b>6</b>. You will use trace tables in every lesson of this course.</p>
      </div>
</div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="errors">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · When code fails</p>
      <h2>Three kinds of errors</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
        <p>Errors come in three types. Knowing which one you have tells you where to look:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Syntax error</b></td><td>The code breaks Java's grammar, so it will not compile.</td><td>a missing <code>;</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Runtime error</b></td><td>The program starts, then crashes.</td><td>using an index that does not exist</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Logic error</b></td><td>The program runs, but the answer is wrong.</td><td>a wrong condition</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="remember"><p class="note-label">A simple debugging habit</p><p>Read the exact error message. Trace a small example by hand. Change one thing, then test again.</p></div>
          </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-refresher" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li><code>=</code> stores: work out the right side, then store it in the variable on the left.</li>
          <li>Conditions choose a path; loops repeat; methods package one task with inputs and an output.</li>
          <li>Array indexes start at 0, so <code>arr[2]</code> is the third value.</li>
          <li>A trace table shows how each variable changes, one step at a time.</li>
          <li>Syntax errors stop compiling, runtime errors crash, logic errors give wrong answers.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/intro/'}>Introduction to DSA</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
